_abstract:_ The STAR experiment at RHIC studies extreme states of matter which forms in the nucleus-nucleus collisions at energies \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\) GeV. Jet production and modification of their shape when passing through the hot bulk matter represent one of the most important probes of this matter. In order to quantify these modifications, it is important to compare these measurements with control measurements in the d+Au collisions.

The main aim of this work is the application of modern jet-reconstruction algorithms on the data from d+Au collisions at the STAR experiment, with emphasis on measurement of jet \(j_{\rm T}\) distributions. Furthermore, these algorithms will be applied on simulated data in order to determine corrections for the measured spectra.

_key words:_ jets, STAR, \(j_{\rm T}\), jet reconstruction algorithms

