### Jet \(j_{\rm T}\)

Projection of the particle's momentum into the plane perpendicular to the jet axis is called \(j_{\rm T}\). Jet \(j_{\rm T}\) is to the jet axis as the \(p_{\rm T}\) of a particle is to the beam axis. The \(j_{\rm T}\) is an experimentally robust quantity - it is insensitive to measurement of the jet energy and depends only on the jet axis determination, since the particle momentum is measured quite precisely by the TPC.

#### Raw \(j_{\rm T}\)

Based on the raw \(p_{\rm T}\) distributions we have selected jets with transverse momentum 6-7 GeV/\(c\) from minimum-bias data sample and 15-20 GeV/\(c\) from HT0. Numbers of particles in these jets are shown on Figures 4.5 - 4.8.

Figures 4.9, 4.10, 4.11 and 4.12 show the uncorrected \(j_{\rm T}\) distributions of "minimum-bias" and "high-tower" jets. The distributions are plotted up to the value of \(j_{\rm T}^{max}=p_{\rm T}^{cut}\cdot\sin R\), since larger \(j_{\rm T}\)s are biased by the resolution parameter \(R\). Therefore the \(j_{\rm T}\) distributions for \(p_{\rm T}^{cut}=0.6\) GeV/\(c\) end already at \(j_{\rm T}\)=0.3 GeV/\(c\). All the \(j_{\rm T}\) distributions are for charged particles only, since the momentum of individual neutral hadrons is not available.

