The final step is to calculate \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\). With the use of Equation 4.5, one gets the following results:

\[\begin{array}{ll}&\mbox{\bf jets 6-7 GeV/c, MB:}\\ &R=0.5,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=0.6\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=480\pm 80\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &R=0.5,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=460\pm 30\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &R=0.7,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=0.6\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=400\pm 20\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &R=0.7,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=470\pm 50\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &\mbox{\bf jets 15-20 GeV/c, HT0:}\\ &R=0.5,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=0.6\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=470\pm 60\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &R=0.5,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=520\pm 30\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &R=0.7,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=0.6\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=420\pm 30\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ &R=0.7,\,p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\ {\rm GeV/}c\quad\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2} \rangle}=600\pm 30\ {\rm(stat.)}\ {\rm MeV/}c\\ \end{array}\]

Statistical errors are determined by the errors of \(\sigma\) and coefficients \(C_{jT}\).

However, as shown in Table 4.2, fits for minimum-bias data with cuts \(R=0.5\), \(p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\) GeV/\(c\) and \(R=0.7\), \(p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\) GeV/\(c\), have \(\chi^{2}\)/NDF significantly greater than 1. It is reasonable question, if resulting values of \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) are meaningful. It is also possible to calculate \(\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle\) straight from the histogram:

\[\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle=\frac{\sum_{n=1}^{N}c(n)\cdot j_{ \rm T}^{3}(n)}{\sum_{n=1}^{N}c(n)\cdot j_{\rm T}(n)} \tag{4.6}\]

where \(N\) is the total number of bins in the histogram, \(c(n)\) is content of the \(n\)-th bin, \(j_{\rm T}(n)\) is the center of the \(n\)-th bin.

From this calculation one gets \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=460\pm 40\) MeV/\(c\) and \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=490\pm 50\) MeV/\(c\) for the minimum-bias data with cuts \(R=0.5\), \(p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\) GeV/\(c\) and \(R=0.7\), \(p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\) GeV/\(c\) respectively. These values are in a good agreement with the original results \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=460\pm 40\) MeV/\(c\) and \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=470\pm 50\) MeV/\(c\).

One can see that values of \(\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle\) for the same \(p_{\rm T}\) cut for both values of \(R\) are in an agreement in the range of statistical errors, which is what would one expect, since we have made a correction for the jet size. The only exception are high-tower jets with \(p_{\rm T}^{cut}=2.0\) GeV/\(c\). The systematic errors have not been estimated yet and are a subject of further studies.. It is possible that after the calculation of the systematic errors also these two values will get into an agreement.

#### Comparison to other \(j_{\rm T}\) measurements

This analysis is partially based on the analysis made by Thomas Henry [23] using d+Au data from Run 03 (2003). However, several significant improvements have been made in my analysis:

* there is approximately five times more events in both Run 08 minimum-bias and HT0 data than in Run 03
* in 2003 BEMC covered only \(0<\eta<1\), while in 2008 the BEMC acceptance was \(-1<\eta<1\). Therefore Henry was forced to use \(R=0.5\).
* advanced reconstruction algorithms (instead of a cone algorithm) in combination with background energy subtraction based on the anti-\(k_{\rm T}\) algorithm.

Henry's results are following: for 6 - 7 GeV/\(c\) jets \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=630\pm 12\) (stat.) \(\pm 30\) (syst.) MeV/\(c\) and for range 15 - 20 GeV/\(c\)\(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=630\pm 13\) (stat.) \(\pm 30\) (syst.) MeV/\(c\) for d+Au data. He also analyzed p+p data at \(\sqrt{s}=200\) GeV with the results (comparable with d+Au) \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=690\pm 12\) (stat.) \(\pm 60\) (syst.) MeV/\(c\) for 6-7 GeV/\(c\) jets \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=573\pm 9\) (stat.) \(\pm 30\) (syst.) MeV/\(c\) for 15-20 GeV/\(c\) jets.

In 1980 the CCOR Collaboration at ISR [24] at CERN measured \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=697\pm 13\) MeV/\(c\) for three various collision energies: \(\sqrt{s}=31,45,62\) GeV.

The PHENIX Collaboration at RHIC [25] calculated \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) from di-hadron correlations in p+p collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=200\) GeV. Their result is \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}=585\pm 5\) (stat.) \(\pm 15\) (syst.) MeV/\(c\).

Despite the fact that the CCOR Collaboration and the PHENIX Collaboration measured \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) from di-hadron correlations and not for jets, it shows up in all measurements that \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) doesn't significantly change with jet energy and that the results are consistent for both ISR and RHIC collision energy ranges. It also shows up that p+p results are comparable with d+Au \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) values.

## Summary and Outlook

In this work, the first results on \(j_{T}\) distributions for jets from d+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\)=200 GeV measured by the STAR Collaboration in Run8 at RHIC are presented. For the jet reconstruction, modern jet reconstruction algorithms \(k_{\rm T}\) and anti-\(k_{\rm T}\) were used.

High-tower trigger bias was shown to be negligible and detector effects have been corrected using PYTHIA and GEANT simulation.

Values of \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) have been calculated for various jet energies. It seems that \(\sqrt{\langle j_{\rm T}^{2}\rangle}\) doesn't significantly change with rising jet energy and with \(p_{\rm T}\) cut imposed on jet particles. Obtained values of \(j_{\rm T}\) are about 25% lower than other measurements at RHIC, however systematic errors of the measurement have not been evaluated yet and are subject of further detailed studies.

The next important step will be an extension of the \(j_{\rm T}\) measurements to Au+Au collisions, in which properties of jets are expected to be modified due to the presence of hot and dense nuclear matter. Experimentally this measurement presents a challenge due to a large and fluctuating underlying event background.

